WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.450
Knitting cables on the knitting
machine can be a challenge.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.920
The crossing of the stitches can be
difficult, plus adding purl stitches

00:00:09.986 --> 00:00:13.330
for a hand knit look can be tedious.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.690
Now, I've always considered using
the ribber to add purl stitches,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.920
but I'd never tried it.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.240
Here are the results of my
experiment with this idea.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.280
I set up my knitting with four needles
in work on the main bed and two

00:00:28.346 --> 00:00:30.410
needles in work on the ribber.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.520
I'm planning a two by two cable with two
purl stitches between each cable.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.320
Remember that when we're crossing cables
on the machine, we're stretching those

00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:46.770
stitches considerably, relying on the
stretch of the yarn to make the cross.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.810
You may even notice that my needles
are being pulled out of position.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.770
And this is just a simple
two by two cable.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.800
Nothing fancy.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.040
What I found is that no matter how I
held the transfer tools, no matter what

00:01:03.106 --> 00:01:07.130
angle I held them, this is clumsy at best.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.760
Plus, the stitches on the ribber are very
tight and tend to pop off and misform.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.000
Try it yourself, but I'll
bet you have similar results.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.480
My conclusion with this experiment is
that I'll cross cables on the single bed

00:01:23.546 --> 00:01:26.530
and relatch purl stitches that I want.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.200
Or, better yet, leave cables
for hand knitting projects.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.160
What do you think?

